summary:
We built an interactive 3D route planner for the first astronauts on Mars, set in Gale Crater on the real terrain Curiosity has explored since 2012. It combines NASA and partner data in one map:

a 1 m elevation model and HiRISE imagery;
CRISM minerals;
RAD radiation and REMS weather;
Curiosity’s 38 km traverse with its 49 drill sites.

The tool plans a two-astronaut expedition. The crew starts at a habitat placed by our own suitability analysis, away from hazards and from Curiosity’s historic tracks, and travels in a pressurized rover. One astronaut walks out at each science stop. For every leg the tool shows the safest route for the vehicle and for the astronaut, the slope against suit limits, possible sand, the accumulated radiation dose, the time back to shelter, and the scientific reason for the stop.

This addresses the challenge by turning multi-mission data into survival decisions. It matters because a rover takes weeks to cover what a person could cross in one outing, but a person faces new limits. Every value is labelled as measured, calculated or assumed.
